DependencyGraph: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the dependency graph model — how dependencies are represented,
  validated, and evaluated. Reusable system intelligence; concrete
  project graphs live in projects/{project_name}/dependencies/graph.sdd.

Owns:
  Dependency graph model, DAG rules, topological sort, cycle detection,
  readiness calculation, failure propagation.

Principles:
  Directed: edges have direction A → B (B depends on A)
  Acyclic: graph MUST be a DAG; cycles are forbidden
  Dependency is constraint, not sequence: scheduler derives order
  Readiness is derived: computed from dependency satisfaction, not stored
  Failure propagates: according to dependency type policy

DependencyTypes:
  BLOCKING: target cannot start until source satisfies condition
  REQUIRED: same as BLOCKING; source must satisfy or target rejected
  CONDITIONAL: active only when condition met; otherwise ignored
  OPTIONAL: target can start without source; failure does not block
  SOFT: target can start even if source fails; warning logged
  RESOURCE: target depends on resource availability

Readiness:
  ready(task) =
    all(dependencies.satisfied)
    AND state in [NEW, PLANNED, BLOCKED]
    AND not_locked
    AND policy_allows

  dependency.satisfied =
    type in [BLOCKING, REQUIRED]:
      source.state in [COMPLETED, SKIPPED] AND condition true
    type == CONDITIONAL:
      condition false → satisfied (ignored)
      condition true → same as BLOCKING
    type in [OPTIONAL, SOFT]:
      always satisfied
    type == RESOURCE:
      resource.available == true

FailurePropagation:
  BLOCKING/REQUIRED → target BLOCKED
  CONDITIONAL → re-evaluate condition
  OPTIONAL/SOFT → target remains READY (warning logged)
  RESOURCE → target BLOCKED until resource recovers

CriticalPath:
  longest_path = max(sum(weights) for all paths from root to leaf)
  Critical path tasks cannot be delayed without delaying the project.
  Priority boost: critical path tasks MAY receive higher scheduler priority.

Validation:
  On create:
    - referenced node must exist
    - edge type must be valid
    - self-dependency forbidden (A → A)
    - cross-project dependency requires allow_cross_project = true
    - cycle detection MUST pass
    - policy MUST allow the dependency

  On delete:
    - find all dependents
    - deny if dependents exist, or require approval for cascade

CycleDetection:
  Algorithm: DFS-based cycle detection on directed graph.
  On adding edge A → B: run DFS from B; if A reachable → REJECT.

TopologicalSort:
  Algorithm: Kahn's algorithm or DFS-based topological sort.
  Output: ordered list where every node appears after all dependencies.

Rules:
  [DG1] Dependency graph MUST be a DAG. Cycles MUST be rejected.
  [DG2] Self-dependency (A → A) is FORBIDDEN.
  [DG3] Cross-project dependencies require explicit allow_cross_project and policy approval.
  [DG4] Dependency validation MUST run before any edge is added.
  [DG5] Readiness is derived from dependency satisfaction, not stored.
  [DG6] Failure propagation follows dependency type policy unless overridden.
  [DG7] Dependency Engine MUST NOT change task state directly.
  [DG8] Topological sort uses stable tie-breaking (task ID, creation time).
  [DG9] Critical path is recalculated when graph changes.
  [DG10] Dependency depth > threshold SHOULD trigger architecture review.

